From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: tell the DMA layer how far the IDMAC can address

The IDMAC descriptors carry word addresses. The A733 manual defines DES2
(buffer address, Table 11-7), DES3 (next descriptor, Table 11-8) and SMHC_DLBA
(register 0x0084) each as a word address (4 bytes), so a variant with
idma_des_shift = 2 -- which is how this driver already writes those fields --
reaches 34 bits of physical memory through 32-bit registers.

Nothing tells the DMA layer that. A platform device created from OF starts at
DMA_BIT_MASK(32), and of_dma_configure() only ever narrows a mask, never
widens it, so the controller is treated as a 32-bit device. On a board whose
DRAM starts at 0x1_00000000 that means every single transfer is bounced
through swiotlb: a memcpy per byte, on top of each request, on the CPU.

Measured on the Radxa Cubie A7S, the 8 GiB board, same script and same card on both
stacks:

  vendor 6.6.98   ondemand, unpinned   0.83 s / 256 MiB   323 MB/s
  vendor 6.6.98   performance, cpu6    0.82 s / 256 MiB   327 MB/s
  ours   6.18.36  schedutil, unpinned  2.20 s / 256 MiB   122 MB/s
  ours   6.18.36  performance, cpu6    1.82 s / 256 MiB   148 MB/s

Two fingerprints of the bounce, both from silicon rather than from reading the
vendor source. io_tlb_used_hiwater moves from 512 to 1024 slabs across our
reads -- 2 MiB, exactly max_req_size -- while the vendor kernel's stays at 309
for the whole run. And our read gains 21 % from raising the CPU clock from
247 MHz to 2002 MHz where the vendor's gains 1 %, which is what a data path
that copies every byte on the CPU looks like next to one that does not.

So set the mask from the shift the variant already declares. The A733 asks for
34 bits, which covers this board's DRAM whole; every variant without
idma_des_shift asks for the 32 bits it already had, so nothing changes for
them. The vendor driver hardcodes DMA_BIT_MASK(64) here, which the hardware
does not back up -- it only survives because 8 GiB of DRAM at 0x1_00000000
happens to end inside 34 bits.

A platform that cannot honour the wider mask keeps the narrower one and warns:
bounce buffering is slower, not wrong.

Not hardware-tested yet. The test is a cold boot with the same read: if the
bounce is what costs us, io_tlb_used_hiwater stops moving during the read and
the difference between the 247 MHz and 2002 MHz runs collapses.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -1765,6 +1765,33 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The IDMAC descriptors hold word addresses, not byte addresses: the
+	 * A733 manual documents DES2 (buffer), DES3 (next descriptor) and
+	 * SMHC_DLBA alike as a word address (4 bytes). A variant that shifts
+	 * its addresses therefore reaches 32 + idma_des_shift bits of physical
+	 * memory through those 32-bit fields, so say so.
+	 *
+	 * It has to be said, because an OF platform device starts at
+	 * DMA_BIT_MASK(32) and of_dma_configure() only ever narrows that mask,
+	 * never widens it. Left at 32 bits on a board whose DRAM starts at
+	 * 0x1_00000000, every transfer is bounced through swiotlb, which costs
+	 * a memcpy per byte on top of each request. Measured on the Cubie A7S:
+	 * io_tlb_used_hiwater climbs by max_req_size worth of slabs across a
+	 * read where the vendor kernel's does not move, and the same read costs
+	 * us 21 % more wall clock at 247 MHz than at 2002 MHz while the vendor
+	 * is indifferent to CPU frequency.
+	 *
+	 * Variants without idma_des_shift ask for the 32 bits they already had.
+	 * If the platform cannot honour the wider mask, keep going: the 32-bit
+	 * path with bounce buffering is slower, not wrong.
+	 */
+	ret = dma_set_mask_and_coherent(&pdev->dev,
+					DMA_BIT_MASK(32 + host->cfg->idma_des_shift));
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(&pdev->dev, "cannot use %u-bit DMA, staying at 32\n",
+			 32 + host->cfg->idma_des_shift);
+
 	host->sg_cpu = dma_alloc_coherent(&pdev->dev, PAGE_SIZE,
 					  &host->sg_dma, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!host->sg_cpu)
